# 1. The document, and the two things beside it

An `.ndo` file is prose with fenced blocks. A block tagged `turbulance` is a
**cell**. Running the document compiles each cell and writes an `output` block
directly below it.

Nothing here is a notebook kernel. There is no live session between commands:
every run builds a fresh interpreter and replays the document from cell 0.

## Run this file

    ndombolo run 01-first-document.ndo

Then look at what changed on disk. Three things exist now, and it matters that
you can tell them apart.

## The first cell

```turbulance
item greeting = "hello"
print(greeting)
```

Run it and an output block appears below the fence. That block is a **view of
the last run** -- run it again and it is replaced, not appended.

## Cells share a store

A later cell sees what an earlier one left. That is why `run` always replays
from the top: cell 1's meaning depends on the store cell 0 built.

```turbulance
item names = ["ndombolo", "turbulance"]
item n = len(names)
print(greeting, n)
```

`greeting` was never declared in this cell. It is in scope because cell 0 ran
first.

## The record is not the output

Beside the file sits `01-first-document.ndo.record` -- a counter that only ever
goes up. Clearing an output block removes the *view*; the deposits that produced
it stay.

    ndombolo record 01-first-document.ndo
    ndombolo clear  01-first-document.ndo
    ndombolo record 01-first-document.ndo

Run those three in order. The `clear` prints how many output blocks it removed
and then prints the record, which has not moved. Re-run the document and the
record advances again -- past where it was, never back to it.

There is no undo. `clear` is itself a thing that happened.

## Reading the document without running it

    ndombolo cells 01-first-document.ndo

Lists each cell with its line and first line of source. It compiles nothing, so
a half-written cell is listed like any other.

## The whole command surface

    ndombolo run    <file> [--cell N]   run cells, write outputs back
    ndombolo clear  <file> [--cell N]   remove output blocks
    ndombolo cells  <file>              list cells without running
    ndombolo graph  <file>              the script graph, as JSON
    ndombolo trace  <file>              every event, as JSON
    ndombolo record <file>              the deposits behind this file
    ndombolo new    <file>              write a starter document
    ndombolo edit   <file>              open it in the editor

`--cell N` selects what is **written**, not what is run. With `--cell 1`, cell 0
still runs -- it has to, to build the store -- but only cell 1's output block is
replaced. The earlier run still deposits, which is why the record advances by
two.

Try it:

    ndombolo clear 01-first-document.ndo
    ndombolo run   01-first-document.ndo --cell 1

Only the second cell gets an output block. The record moved by two.

